<header><p class="st-kicker">sultanate · Maritime Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Pattani Kingdom</h1><p class="st-meta">1350 CE – 1902 CE (approx.) · capital Pattani</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Malay sultanate on the east coast of the peninsula, famous for its 17th-century queens and annexed by Siam in 1902.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Pattani grew as the successor to the old <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/langkasuka">Langkasuka</a> trading region. According to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hikayat-patani">Hikayat Patani</a>, its ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phaya-tu-nakpa">Phaya Tu Nakpa</a> accepted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> in the mid-15th century, taking the name <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> Ismail Shah, and the port rose as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malacca">Malacca's</a> trade expanded. It was nominally a tributary of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/ayutthaya-kingdom">Ayutthaya</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>From 1584 Pattani was ruled for a century by four queens, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raja-hijau">Raja Hijau</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raja-biru">Raja Biru</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raja-ungu">Raja Ungu</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raja-kuning">Raja Kuning</a>. Chinese, Japanese, Portuguese and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> merchants traded there; the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/dutch-east-india-company-territories">Dutch East India Company</a> opened a factory in 1602 and the English in 1612. Pattani was also a centre of Islamic learning, producing scholars such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daud-al-fatani">Daud al-Fatani</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Siam conquered Pattani in 1786 and split it into seven provinces in the early 19th century. In 1902 it annexed the area fully and deposed Raja Abdul Kadir Kamaruddin, a settlement <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a> accepted in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-siamese-treaty-of-1909">Anglo-Siamese Treaty of 1909</a>. The legacy shapes the Malay Muslim provinces of southern <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/thailand">Thailand</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/pattani-kingdom.jpg" alt="Pattani Kingdom" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Muffin Wizard · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Patani_Kingdom_(until_1816).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1350 CE – 1902 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>sultanate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Pattani</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Ismail Shah, Raja Hijau, Abdul Kadir Kamaruddin</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Patani Malay</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Thailand</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Patani Sultanate, Kingdom of Patani, Pattani, Sultanate of Patani, Patani, Patani Darussalam</dd></dl></aside></div>
